**Proof.**

Let $O$ be the intersection of lines $AB$ and $CD$ (they meet since $AB\not\parallel CD$). Place the origin at $O$, and let $\mathbf u,\mathbf v$ be unit vectors along lines $AB,CD$. Then for some real $a,b,c,d$,
$$A=a\mathbf u,\quad B=b\mathbf u,\quad C=c\mathbf v,\quad D=d\mathbf v.$$
Set $p=b-a$, $q=d-c$. Then $|AB|=|p|$ and $|CD|=|q|$, so the hypothesis $AB=CD$ gives $|p|=|q|$, i.e. $p=\varepsilon q$ for $\varepsilon\in\{+1,-1\}$.

The midpoints of the diagonals are
$$E=\tfrac12(a\mathbf u+c\mathbf v),\qquad F=\tfrac12\big((a+p)\mathbf u+(c+q)\mathbf v\big),$$
so line $EF$ has direction
$$F-E=\tfrac12(p\mathbf u+q\mathbf v),\qquad\text{i.e. } EF\parallel \mathbf w:=p\mathbf u+q\mathbf v.$$
Since $p=\varepsilon q$, $\mathbf w=q(\varepsilon\mathbf u+\mathbf v)$. If $\varepsilon=+1$, $\mathbf w\parallel\mathbf u+\mathbf v$ (internal bisector of lines $AB,CD$); if $\varepsilon=-1$, $\mathbf w\parallel\mathbf u-\mathbf v$ (external bisector). In either case $EF$ is **parallel to an angle bisector** of the two lines $AB$ and $CD$.

Now locate $G,H$. Write $G=g\mathbf u$ (since $G\in AB$) and $G=E+t(F-E)=\tfrac12((a+tp)\mathbf u+(c+tq)\mathbf v)$. The $\mathbf v$-component vanishes: $c+tq=0$, so $t=-c/q$, giving
$$g=\frac{aq-cp}{2q}.\quad\text{Likewise } H=h\mathbf v,\quad h=-\frac{aq-cp}{2p}.$$
Set $K:=aq-cp$; then $g=K/(2q)$, $h=-K/(2p)$.

Let $s=\mathbf u\cdot\mathbf v=\cos\theta$ where $\theta=\angle(\mathbf u,\mathbf v)\in(0,\pi)$ (nonzero since $AB\not\parallel CD$). We have
$$\overrightarrow{GA}=(a-g)\mathbf u,\quad \overrightarrow{GH}=h\mathbf v-g\mathbf u,\quad \overrightarrow{HD}=(d-h)\mathbf v,\quad \overrightarrow{HG}=g\mathbf u-h\mathbf v,$$
with $d=c+q$. Since $|\overrightarrow{GH}|=|\overrightarrow{HG}|$,
$$\cos\angle AGH=\frac{(a-g)(hs-g)}{|a-g|\,|\overrightarrow{GH}|},\qquad \cos\angle DHG=\frac{(d-h)(gs-h)}{|d-h|\,|\overrightarrow{GH}|}.$$

A direct substitution using $g=K/(2q),\ h=-K/(2p),\ p=\varepsilon q$ gives
$$hs-g=-\frac{K(s+\varepsilon)}{2p},\qquad gs-h=\frac{K(1+\varepsilon s)}{2p}.$$
- If $\varepsilon=+1$: $\;gs-h=-(hs-g)$.
- If $\varepsilon=-1$: $\;gs-h=hs-g$.

It remains to fix the signs of $a-g$ and $d-h$. Because $G$ lies on segment $AB$ and $H$ on segment $CD$:

*Case $\varepsilon=+1$ ($p=q$).* If $p>0$: $G\in[a,a+p]\Rightarrow a-g\le 0$, and $H\in[c,c+p]\Rightarrow d-h\ge 0$, so $\operatorname{sgn}(a-g)=-\operatorname{sgn}(d-h)$. (The case $p<0$ reverses both signs but keeps them opposite.)

*Case $\varepsilon=-1$ ($p=-q$).* If $p>0$ (so $q<0$): $G\in[a,a+p]\Rightarrow a-g\le 0$, and $H\in[c+q,c]\Rightarrow d-h\le 0$, so $\operatorname{sgn}(a-g)=\operatorname{sgn}(d-h)$. (Again $p<0$ is analogous.)

These are precisely the sign relations needed in each case, yielding
$$\operatorname{sgn}(a-g)\cdot(hs-g)=\operatorname{sgn}(d-h)\cdot(gs-h),$$
hence $\cos\angle AGH=\cos\angle DHG$. Since both angles lie in $(0,\pi)$, we conclude
$$\angle AGH=\angle DHG. \qquad\blacksquare$$

